WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.150
Every user will sign in differently.

2
00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:04.375
Federal policy sets the target.

3
00:00:04.375 --> 00:00:10.007
OMB's zero trust memo and NIST's authentication
guideline require phishing-resistant

4
00:00:10.007 --> 00:00:13.762
sign-in for staff, contractors
and partners, and passwords,

5
00:00:13.762 --> 00:00:16.813
one-time codes and push
approvals don't qualify.

6
00:00:16.813 --> 00:00:21.252
So users learn a PIV card, Windows
Hello for Business or a passkey,

7
00:00:21.252 --> 00:00:23.849
with at least two methods registered.

8
00:00:23.849 --> 00:00:29.186
The course has six short modules:
sign-in; reset and recovery; devices;

9
00:00:29.186 --> 00:00:34.713
where files now live; reporting; and
what the help desk will and won't ask.

10
00:00:34.713 --> 00:00:39.249
Password lessons teach length and
passphrases, not character classes.

11
00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:41.081
The last module matters most,

12
00:00:41.081 --> 00:00:45.125
because the help desk becomes a
target the moment users trust it.

13
00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:50.263
CISA's Scattered Spider advisory
describes attackers posing as I T staff.

14
00:00:50.263 --> 00:00:54.988
The real help desk never asks for a
password, a PIN or a code, never asks you to

15
00:00:54.988 --> 00:00:56.615
approve a prompt for them,

16
00:00:56.615 --> 00:01:00.170
and never sends a pass to an
address you give on the call.
